WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.647
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.647 --> 00:00:11.404
I was working at a nonprofit called
Generation when I met Jasmine.

00:00:12.998 --> 00:00:18.950
Jasmine was only 24 years old, but
she had already been through a lot and

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:23.180
life kept throwing her
curveball after curveball.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.050
She had held a few jobs off and
on, but most were part-time and

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.018
it was hard to manage her work schedule
with caring for her one year-old daughter.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.810
She was living with her
daughter's father for a while, so

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.239
that provided some stability.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.560
But unfortunately he developed
an alcohol abuse problem, and

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.040
she began to fear for
own safety and her daughter's.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:54.400
Jasmine found a community in Atlanta
that provides a safe haven for

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.819
women who have escaped abusive situations.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.390
With the solid footing of housing and
safety, she began to look for

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.880
full time work, determined to create
a better world for her daughter.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.510
One that held the promise of
opportunity that she never had.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.160
Jasmine's determination was admirable.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.470
She applied to multiple jobs every day,
but

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.230
received interviews from
very few employers.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.340
The interviews she had,
they weren't going so well either.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.926
She knew someone had to be hiring, but

00:01:28.926 --> 00:01:33.726
despite her best efforts,
no one wanted to hire Jasmine.

00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:38.063
This story is not uncommon.

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:42.433
What I found from my work at Generation
is that while the details may change,

00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:47.460
similar circumstances are experienced
by many unemployed young people.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.370
Now, when I say unemployed young people,
I mean those aged 18 to 29 who

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.840
are not in school and
are actively searching for work.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.760
The International Labor Organization
estimates that around the world,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.050
75 million young people are unemployed.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.920
Three times as many are underemployed.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.810
This represents not just a source
of individual despair, but

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:16.820
also a source of social unrest, as well
as a gigantic pool of untapped talent.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.120
Paradoxically, this is
a two-sided problem.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.750
According to a global report
by McKinsey &amp; Company,

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:30.940
40% of employers say that a skill shortage
is driving entry-level vacancies.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.350
Meaning, employers cannot find the skills
that they need in the labor pool.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:42.369
This problem has persisted for years, but
it is only now beginning to be addressed.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.740
The solution I proposed,
modeled on this issue,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.330
is to create partnerships between
the nonprofit and for-profit worlds

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:55.890
that from the outset align outcomes and
enable stronger collaboration.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.920
I believe this proposition is valuable
in many sectors, but for the purpose of

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.780
illustration, let's consider this issue
of unemployment among young people.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.350
There are three critical aspects of
the education-to-employment journey.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.248
First, enrollment.

00:03:12.248 --> 00:03:16.345
Are enough young people being trained for
the job market?

00:03:16.345 --> 00:03:18.922
Two, building skills.

00:03:18.922 --> 00:03:22.470
Is training giving young
people the right skills?

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.990
And three, finding a job.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.660
Can young job seekers find open positions?

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.720
So why does it not work for
nonprofits to solve this issue alone?

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.160
A nonprofit that focuses on skills
training has many priorities.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.030
With limited time and
resources, attending to this

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.150
entire education-to-employment
journey is nearly impossible.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.600
And there are outstanding questions
on all three of these aspects.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.697
First, enrollment.

00:03:52.697 --> 00:03:55.451
The nonprofit sector is highly fragmented,
and

00:03:55.451 --> 00:04:00.022
with a donation-based business model,
nonprofits cannot operate at the scale

00:04:00.022 --> 00:04:04.480
needed to serve all of the young people
who would benefit from their services.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.342
Two, building skills.

00:04:08.342 --> 00:04:12.546
Without employer involvement,
there is no mechanism to ensure that

00:04:12.546 --> 00:04:16.966
the curriculum and delivery is done in
a way that prepares young people to

00:04:16.966 --> 00:04:20.240
be successful on the job,
not just in the classroom.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.560
And third, finding a job.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.947
Without commitments from, or at least
connections with employers, nonprofits

00:04:28.947 --> 00:04:34.346
may not be helping young people with the
critical piece of actually landing a job.

00:04:36.924 --> 00:04:38.606
So now, let's shift gears and

00:04:38.606 --> 00:04:42.760
consider the other side of the coin,
the for-profit world.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.520
In this case, that means employers.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.730
Why do employers not
solve this issue alone?

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.480
Why don't they just hire young people,
and then train them?

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.640
The problem is, it's expensive.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.990
The employer would have to invest
in developing the right type of

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.560
skills training.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.090
And then when they hire employees, these
employees spend a long time being trained.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.760
And during that time, they're not
producing output for the company.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.620
Some large employers do have the capacity
to develop training programs.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.530
But the reality is, most unemployed young
people aren't going to be working for

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.601
these resource-rich large companies.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:26.300
Additionally, many unemployed young people
come from difficult life situations,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.700
similar to Jasmine.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.230
They need social support
structures in place

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.210
to help them succeed in their training and
in their transition to the workplace.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.949
And this is why nonprofits
are also needed as part of

00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:41.197
the equation to address the social
issues of unemployment,

00:05:41.197 --> 00:05:45.103
not just the talent development piece
in which employers are invested.

00:05:48.278 --> 00:05:53.406
In 2015, Generation, where I worked, was
launched to bring together the nonprofit

00:05:53.406 --> 00:05:58.010
and for-profit worlds to solve this issue
of unemployment among young people.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.590
When Jasmine went through
the Generation Program,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.460
she benefited tremendously
from this model.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:05.660
As a nonprofit,

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.217
Generation was able to provide social
support services such as a mentor, and

00:06:10.217 --> 00:06:13.505
a stipend to help her recover,
her transportation, and food cost.

00:06:14.595 --> 00:06:17.750
The program's emphasis on employer
involvement was also key to

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.565
Jasmine's success.

00:06:19.565 --> 00:06:23.571
For instance, Generation had worked
directly with employers from

00:06:23.571 --> 00:06:27.585
the beginning to identify the key
skills and mindsets they needed.

00:06:27.585 --> 00:06:29.965
And this was the foundation for
Jasmine's training.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:35.760
Generation has focused on proving
the positive return on investment of

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.420
the program.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.840
As this return on investment is proven,
Generation's funding sources have become

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:48.590
diverse, and include governments,
foundations, and employers themselves.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:51.720
All stakeholders benefit
from this collaboration.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.250
Governments reduce their
unemployment rate,

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:58.030
foundations provide economic mobility for
young people, and

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:03.770
employers have a pipeline of talent with
reduced turnover and higher productivity.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:08.965
This collaboration is essential to reach
the scale needed to truly move the needle

00:07:08.965 --> 00:07:10.665
on unemployment among young people.

00:07:12.435 --> 00:07:14.985
When Jasmine graduated from
the Generation Program,

00:07:14.985 --> 00:07:17.305
she received the job of her dreams.

00:07:17.305 --> 00:07:18.085
She called me and

00:07:18.085 --> 00:07:22.755
said something to the effect of,
I feel like my life has changed already.

00:07:23.915 --> 00:07:27.360
I want my daughter,
when she's grown, to look back and

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:28.680
be proud of the life we made.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.166
I think that's possible now.

00:07:33.339 --> 00:07:36.140
The program worked for Jasmine.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:40.970
The program has worked for
over 25,000 young people in

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.670
99 cities across 9 countries,
and it's expanding rapidly.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.160
I had the opportunity to help launch
the Generation Hong Kong program

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.460
in Spring 2018,
right before coming to Stanford.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.296
Here's a photo of their
first graduation ceremony.

00:08:00.548 --> 00:08:05.482
The program is impactful and scalable,
and this would not be possible

00:08:05.482 --> 00:08:10.594
without the collaboration among
sectors in each Generation location.

00:08:13.443 --> 00:08:17.862
Today I've told you a story about
unemployment among young adults,

00:08:17.862 --> 00:08:21.070
because it's an issue that
matters deeply to me.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.390
But I'm also excited to be
sharing my story with you

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.530
because I want to extend an invitation.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:35.040
I invite you to think about an issue
that matters deeply to you.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:41.610
It could be malnutrition, financial
insecurity, access to healthcare,

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.140
or any other issue that moves you.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:48.659
What would be possible if nonprofit and

00:08:48.659 --> 00:08:53.824
for-profit organizations worked
hand in hand every step of the way,

00:08:53.824 --> 00:08:58.025
like they've done for Jasmine and
like they've done for

00:08:58.025 --> 00:09:02.688
thousands of other unemployed
young people around the world?

00:09:02.688 --> 00:09:08.107
I challenge you to extend this model
to the issue that matters to you,

00:09:08.107 --> 00:09:12.780
because there are so
many more people in our communities and

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.504
around the world that we can help.

00:09:15.504 --> 00:09:16.438
Thank you.

00:09:16.438 --> 00:09:20.635
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:20.635 --> 00:09:25.382
[MUSIC]

